## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an optimized DP on a circular constraint and track the number of optimal solutions.

## Key Concepts
- DP on linear sequences with spacing (dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[i-d] + v[i]))
- Reducing circularity to two linear cases (include first vs exclude first)
- Counting distinct optimal subsets with tie handling (sum counts when equal)

## Code Example
```javascript
function linearDP(arr, d){
  const n = arr.length; const dp = Array(n+1).fill(0); const ways = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 0; ways[0] = 1;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    const take = arr[i-1] + (i-d>=0 ? dp[i-d] : 0);
    const skip = dp[i-1];
    if(take>skip){ dp[i]=take; ways[i]=(i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1); }
    else if(take<skip){ dp[i]=skip; ways[i]=ways[i-1]; }
    else { dp[i]=skip; ways[i] = ((i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1) + ways[i-1]); }
  }
  return [dp[n], ways[n]];
}

function maxCircular(v, d){
  const [bestA, cntA] = linearDP(v.slice(1), d); // exclude first
  const n = v.length;
  const seg = (n > 2*d-1) ? v.slice(d, n-d+1) : [];
  const [bestBseg, cntBseg] = seg.length ? linearDP(seg, d) : [0,1];
  const bestB = v[0] + bestBseg;
  if(bestA > bestB) return [bestA, cntA];
  if(bestB > bestA) return [bestB, cntBseg];
  return [bestA, cntA + cntBseg];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow a variable distance per pair or weighted distance penalties?
- How would you test correctness and performance on worst-case inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates DP on selecting exact number of non-overlapping subarrays with reconstruction, common in profit/segmentation tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays
- Exact k selections
- Prefix sums for efficient range sums
- Backpointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length; const pre = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pre[i] = pre[i-1]+A[i-1];
  const neg inf = -1e18;
  let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf));
  let best = new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf);
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++) best[t] = neg_inf;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      best[t] = Math.max(best[t], dp[i-1][t-1] - pre[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], pre[i] + best[t]);
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most-k windows instead of exactly k?
- How would you reconstruct the actual windows efficiently and handle negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over multiple segments with a budget and in/out state tracking to allow adjacency.

## Key Concepts
- DP with segment count and in/out state
- Transitions for starting, extending, and ending a segment
- Handling adjacency and at-most-k constraint

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(a, k){
  const n = a.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  // dp[i][t][s] -> max sum using first i elements
  let dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> [NEG, NEG]));
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      for(let s=0;s<2;s++){
        const cur = dp[i][t][s];
        if(cur===NEG) continue;
        if(s===0){
          // skip a[i]
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
          // start new
          if(t<k) dp[i+1][t+1][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t+1][1], cur + a[i]);
        } else {
          // extend
          dp[i+1][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][1], cur + a[i]);
          // end
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++) ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n][t][0], dp[n][t][1]);
  return ans;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for array partitioning with a per-segment objective function. It combines partition DP with range maximum queries and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over array partitions
- Range maximum queries (precomputation table M[a][b])
- Path reconstruction using parent pointers
- Time/space complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartition(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length-1; // 1-indexed array
  const M = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));

  // Precompute range maximums
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    M[i][i] = A[i];
    for(let j=i+1; j<=n; j++){
      M[i][j] = Math.max(M[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
    for(let i=t; i<=n; i++){
      for(let j=t-1; j<i; j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + M[j+1][i] - c;
        if(val > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct segments
  const segments = [];
  let curr = n;
  for(let t=k; t>=1; t--){
    const prev = par[curr][t];
    segments.unshift([prev+1, curr]);
    curr = prev;
  }

  return {value: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Optimization Opportunities
- Use sparse table for O(1) range max queries with O(n log n) preprocessing
- Apply divide and conquer optimization if applicable
- Consider rolling array optimization to reduce space to O(n)

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP skills with an additional dimension for tracking turns while handling obstacles and grid boundaries. It verifies understanding of initialization logic, edge cases, and how to aggregate the final answer by summing across valid turn counts.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP over cells, turn count, and last direction
- Turn counting via direction changes during transitions
- Correct handling of obstacles and grid boundaries
- Time/space trade-offs and proper base case logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, K) {
  const m = grid.length;
  const n = grid[0].length;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[m-1][n-1] === 1) return 0;

  const dp = Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [0, 0])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = 1; // right
  dp[0][0][0][1] = 1; // down

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        // from left (coming from right)
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t][0]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t-1][1]; // turn
        }
        // from top (coming from down)
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t][1]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t-1][0]; // turn
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let ans = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    ans += dp[m-1][n-1][t][0] + dp[m-1][n-1][t][1];
  }
  return ans;
}
```

This runs in O(m × n × K) time and O(m × n × K) space.

