## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to model selections of multiple disjoint subarrays with an exact count — a common dynamic programming pattern in interviews.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefix**: State dp[i][t] tracks the best total using the first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) calculation of any subarray sum
- **Backpointers**: Store decisions to reconstruct the chosen subarrays
- **Edge cases**: Handle when n < k or when all values are negative

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skip: true };
      }
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (t > 0 && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t - 1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const p = prev[i][t];
    if (p.skip) { i--; continue; }
    result.push([p.l, i]);
    i = p.l - 1; t = p.t;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: result.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with an extra resource dimension (shields) on a classic grid path. Demonstrates handling of constrained resources, 3D DP states, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an additional dimension for resources (k)
- Transition with/without consuming a shield
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGridPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<m; j++) {
      for (let k=0; k<=K; k++) {
        const v = grid[i][j];
        if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k] + v);
        if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k] + v);
        if (k>0 && v < 0) {
          if (i>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i-1][j][k-1]);
          if (j>0) dp[i][j][k] = Math.max(dp[i][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover one optimal path and its moves?
- How to optimize space to O(n*m*K)?

## Why This Is Asked
Offers a new DP angle: path with exact turns, combining grid path DP with turn accounting. Tests deriving a tight state space, base cases, and reconstruction, mirroring routing-like constraints in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with an extra dimension for turns
- Two direction states to capture turns
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Careful base/edge-case handling (start cell, negative infinities)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  // 4D DP: [i][j][t][d]
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () =>
    Array.from({length: K+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])));
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        // last move right
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(
            (dp[i][j-1][t][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t][0] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
        // last move down
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(
            (dp[i-1][j][t][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t][1] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n-1][m-1][K][0], dp[n-1][m-1][K][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to allow at most k turns and also maximize path length if grid values included negative numbers?
- How would you optimize space to O(nmK) memory or better, and what would be the trade-offs for reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a non-trivial DP over a linear sequence with a non-crossing constraint and reconstruction of the chosen pairs. It balances readability with the need to optimize across many left endpoints efficiently.

## Key Concepts

- Non-crossing pairings on a line
- DP by right endpoint with reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs; potential 1D optimization for i dimension

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonCrossingPairs(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      let best = i>0 ? dp[i-1][t] : 0;
      let bestJ = -1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const left = (j>=1) ? dp[j-1][t-1] : (t-1===0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = left + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if(val > best){ best = val; bestJ = j; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = best;
      take[i][t] = bestJ;
    }
  }
  // reconstruction sketch (omitted for brevity)
  return { value: dp[n-1][k], pairs: [] };
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you enforce exactly k pairs instead of at most k?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n) or derive a monotone queue optimization for certain A distributions?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests advanced dynamic programming techniques involving multiple non-overlapping segments with per-element costs—a pattern frequently appearing in interviews for algorithm-intensive roles.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with fixed number of blocks requiring optimal segmentation
- Prefix sum transformations to simplify recurrence relations
- Time-space optimization strategies and solution reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValue(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i+1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const B = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let x = 0; x <= n; x++) B[x] = P[x] - c * x;

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i][t-1];
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + B[i] - B[j];
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0 && parent[curr][currT] !== -1) {
    const start = parent[curr][currT];
    blocks.unshift([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    currT--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Implementation Details
- The transformation B[x] = P[x] - c*x captures the net value after maintenance costs
- The nested loop structure ensures we consider all possible segment boundaries
- Parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal segment endpoints

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical tree DP approach: building a connected component of fixed size while propagating choices up a rooted tree, including reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Tree knapsack DP for connected subgraphs
- Subtree containment and topmost node principle
- Reconstruction via parent/child pointers
- Time/space: O(N*K^2) in naive merge, with optimizations possible

## Code Example
```javascript
function dfs(u, p) {
  dp[u] = new Array(size[u] + 1).fill(-Infinity);
  dp[u][1] = val[u];
  for (const v of g[u]) if (v !== p) {
    dfs(v, u);
    // merge dp[u] with bestChild from v
    const ndp = new Array(size[u] + size[v] + 1).fill(-Infinity);
    for (let a = 1; a <= size[u]; a++) {
      if (dp[u][a] === -Infinity) continue;
      // option: take nothing from v
      ndp[a] = Math.max(ndp[a], dp[u][a]);
      for (let b = 1; b <= size[v]; b++) {
        if (best[v][b] === -Infinity) continue;
        ndp[a + b] = Math.max(ndp[a + b], dp[u][a] + best[v][b]);
      }
    }
    size[u] += size[v];
    dp[u] = ndp;
  }
}
```

